      "text": "In the traditional Sanskrit grammar of Pāṇini (the *Aṣṭādhyāyī*), noun declensions are fundamentally different from how they are taught in Western textbooks. \n\nWestern grammar teaches dozens of different declension paradigms (a-stems, i-stems, consonant stems, etc.). Pāṇini, however, asserts that **there is only one underlying declension paradigm**. \n\nAccording to Pāṇini, all nouns, pronouns, and adjectives are formed by taking a raw stem (**prātipadika**) and attaching one of 21 underlying suffixes called **sup** affixes. The hundreds of different noun tables we see in modern textbooks are simply the result of morphophonological rules (internal sandhi) reacting to the collision between the end of the stem and the beginning of the *sup* suffix.\n\nHere is a breakdown of all the declensions as per Pāṇini, followed by the edge cases.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Master Template: The 21 *Sup* Affixes\nPāṇini defines the 21 suffixes in a single continuous string in sutra 4.1.2. They are divided into 7 cases (*vibhakti*) and 3 numbers (*vacana*). \n\n| Case (Vibhakti) | Singular (Eka-vacana) | Dual (Dvi-vacana) | Plural (Bahu-vacana) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1st (Prathamā)** / Nom. | **su** (सु) | **au** (औ) | **jas** (जस्) |\n| **2nd (Dvitīyā)** / Acc. | **am** (अम्) | **auṭ** (औट्) | **śas** (शस्) |\n| **3rd (Tṛtīyā)** / Inst. | **ṭā** (टा) | **bhyām** (भ्याम्) | **bhis** (भिस्) |\n| **4th (Caturthī)** / Dat. | **ṅe** (ङे) | **bhyām** (भ्याम्) | **bhyas** (भ्यस्) |\n| **5th (Pañcamī)** / Abl. | **ṅasi** (ङसि) | **bhyām** (भ्याम्) | **bhyas** (भ्यस्) |\n| **6th (Ṣaṣṭhī)** / Gen. | **ṅas** (ङस्) | **os** (ओस्) | **ām** (आम्) |\n| **7th (Saptamī)** / Loc. | **ṅi** (ङि) | **os** (ओस्) | **sup** (सुप्) |\n\n#### Understanding the \"Code\" (Anubandhas)\nPāṇini uses \"dummy letters\" (called *it* or *anubandha*) in these affixes as meta-code to trigger specific grammatical rules. They are dropped before the final word is spoken.\n*   **s**u: The *u* drops. Leaves *s* (which becomes *ḥ* / visarga).\n*   **j**as, **ś**as: The *j* and *ś* drop. Both leave *as*.\n*   au**ṭ**: The *ṭ* drops. Leaves *au*.\n*   **ṭ**ā: The *ṭ* drops. Leaves *ā*.\n*   **ṅ**e, **ṅ**asi, **ṅ**as, **ṅ**i: The *ṅ* drops (as well as the *i* in ṅasi). They leave *e, as, as, i*. \n\n#### What about the 8th Case (Vocative)?\nPāṇini **does not** consider the Vocative (Sambodhana) to be an 8th case. He categorizes it as the 1st Case (Prathamā) with a special designation called *Sambuddhi* applied to the singular. Special rules apply to the singular (like the dropping of the *su* suffix in a-stems: *rāma* instead of *rāmaḥ*), but the dual and plural are identical to the nominative.\n\n---\n\n### 2. How the Template Adapts (The Classes)\nInstead of paradigms, Pāṇini categorizes raw stems (*prātipadika*) by their final letter (*anta*) and gender (*liṅga*). \n\n**By Final Letter:**\n1.  **Ajanta** (Vowel-ending stems): Words ending in *a, ā, i, ī, u, ū, ṛ, ṝ, o, au*.\n2.  **Halanta** (Consonant-ending stems): Words ending in *c, j, t, d, n, s, r*, etc.\n\n**By Gender:**\n1.  **Puṃliṅga** (Masculine)\n2.  **Strīliṅga** (Feminine)\n3.  **Napuṃsakaliṅga** (Neuter) — *Rule: Neuters always substitute the 1st and 2nd case suffixes with specific neuter replacements (like \u0027m\u0027 for singular, \u0027śi\u0027 for plural).*\n\n**Pāṇini\u0027s Stem Dynamics (Aṅga, Pada, Bha):**\nPāṇini states that stems behave differently depending on the suffix following them:\n*   **Strong cases** (*Sarvanāmasthāna*): The first five suffixes (Nom. all, Acc. Sing/Dual). The stem expands or strengthens.\n*   **Weak cases**: Before vowel-initial suffixes, the stem is called **Bha** and often shrinks or loses a vowel.\n*   **Middle cases**: Before consonant-initial suffixes, the stem is called **Pada** and is treated as an independent word, triggering external sandhi rules.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Edge Cases and Exceptions (Apavāda)\nPāṇini handles irregularities through *apavāda* (exception rules) that override the general rules (*utsarga*). The Aṣṭādhyāyī is famous for covering virtually every edge case in the Sanskrit language. \n\nHere are the most notable edge cases:\n\n#### A. The \"Sakhi\" (Friend) and \"Pati\" (Husband) Anomalies\nUsually, masculine nouns ending in *i* (like *muni* or *hari*) belong to a category Pāṇini calls **Ghi**, which standardizes how they interact with suffixes. \n*   **Sakhi (friend)** is explicitly excluded from the *Ghi* class by rule 1.4.7 (*śeṣo ghy asakhi*). It has a highly irregular strong stem (*sakhāyam* instead of *hariṃ*) and weak stem (*sakhyuḥ* instead of *hareḥ*).\n*   **Pati (husband/lord)** is only treated as *Ghi* when it is at the end of a compound (like *prajā-pati*). When standing alone as *pati*, it declines irregularly (e.g., Instrumental singular is *patyā*, not *patinā*).\n\n#### B. The Jackal that Changes Shape (*Kroṣṭu*)\nThe word *kroṣṭu* (jackal) is an *u*-ending noun. However, Pāṇini notes that in the strong cases (Nominative, Accusative), it morphs into an *ṛ*-ending noun (*kroṣṭṛ*), declining exactly like *kartṛ* (creator). E.g., Nom Sing is *kroṣṭā*. In other cases, it toggles optionally between the *u* and *ṛ* stems.\n\n#### C. The Big Four Neuter Body Parts\nPāṇini isolates four neuter *i*-stems that behave wildly in weak cases:\n*   *asthi* (bone)\n*   *dadhi* (yogurt/curds)\n*   *sakthi* (thigh)\n*   *akṣi* (eye)\nIn the weak cases (Instrumental and beyond), they swap their final *i* for *an*. For example, *asthi* becomes *asthnā* (by the bone), not *asthinā*.\n\n#### D. Words by \"Suppletion\" (Complete Base Replacement)\nSometimes an entirely different stem replaces a word in the weak cases. Pāṇini maps these beautifully:\n*   *pāda* (foot) becomes **pad** (e.g., *padbhyām*)\n*   *danta* (tooth) becomes **dat** \n*   *nāsikā* (nose) becomes **nas**\n*   *māṃsa* (meat) becomes **mās**\n*   *hṛdaya* (heart) becomes **hṛd**\n*   *niśā* (night) becomes **niś**\n\n#### E. The Pronouns (Sarvanāman)\nPronouns (like *tad*, *kim*, *sarva*) have their own sub-rules overriding standard *sup* affixes. \n*   In the Dative, *ṅe* becomes *smai* (*tasmai*, *sarvasmai*).\n*   In the Ablative, *ṅasi* becomes *smāt* (*tasmāt*).\n*   In the Locative, *ṅi* becomes *smin* (*tasmin*).\n*   The Genitive plural inserts an *s* instead of an *n* (*teṣām*, not *tenām*).\n\n#### F. Irregular Numerals\nNumbers are treated as plural adjectives.\n*   **Dvi** (two) is exclusively dual.\n*   **Tri** (three) and **Catur** (four) have unique feminine replacements: they become **tisṛ** and **catasṛ** (e.g., *tisṛbhiḥ*, *catasṛṇām*).\n\n#### G. The Word \"Water\" (*Ap*)\nThe word *ap* (water) is an extreme edgecase. It is always feminine and **always plural**. In the Nominative plural it is *āpaḥ*, in the Accusative plural it shrinks to *apaḥ*, and in the Instrumental it takes an anomalous *t* to become *adbhiḥ*.\n\n#### H. Nipātana (Ready-Made Anomalies)\nFor words that are so irregular that generating them via rules would require too many complex steps, Pāṇini uses a mechanism called *nipātana*. He simply lists the finished word in a sutra and says, \"Accept this as grammatically correct as is.\" \nAn example is **anaḍuh** (ox/bull). It takes the affix *num*, turning into *anaḍvān* in the Nominative, *anaḍuhaḥ* in the Accusative plural, and *anaḍudbhiḥ* in the Instrumental.\n\n### Summary\nIf you learn Sanskrit through Western methods, you memorize roughly 30 to 40 different noun tables (paradigms) and their exceptions. Under Pāṇini’s system, you memorize **one table of 21 suffixes** and a logical system of phonological rules, equations, and exceptions (*apavāda*) that automatically generate the thousands of possible noun forms in the language.",
