      "text": "In Pāṇini’s *Aṣṭādhyāyī*, participles act as the bridge between the verb engine and the noun engine. \n\nPāṇini uses a dedicated set of affixes called **Kṛt** to turn a verbal root (*dhātu*) into a participle. Once a *kṛt* affix is attached, Rule 1.2.46 (*kṛttaddhitasamāsāś ca*) triggers, legally classifying the new word as a **Prātipadika** (nominal stem). Because it is now a nominal stem, it is immediately subjected to the exact same 21 *sup* (declension) suffixes we discussed earlier.\n\nBy ignoring sandhi and focusing strictly on how the stems decline, here is how Pāṇini’s engine mechanically generates and declines participles:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Present Active Participles (*Śatṛ* Affix)\nTo say \"going\" or \"eating,\" Pāṇini attaches the affix **śatṛ** to the verb root. The *ś* and *ṛ* are dummy code-letters, leaving the actual suffix **-at**. \n\nThe declension of these participles is highly complex because the stem oscillates between a weak base (**-at**) and a strong base (**-ant**). Pāṇini controls this by injecting the *num* (the letter \u0027n\u0027) augment based on the grammatical case and the verb\u0027s conjugational class (Gaṇa).\n\n*   **The Standard Engine (Classes 1, 4, 10):** In strong cases (Nominative and Accusative singular/dual), Pāṇini commands the insertion of \u0027n\u0027.\n    *   Root *gam* (go) + *śatṛ* \u003d *gacchat*.\n    *   Nom. singular masculine: **gacchan** (goes strong).\n    *   Accusative plural masculine: **gacchataḥ** (stays weak; no \u0027n\u0027).\n*   **The \"Reduplicating\" Exception (Class 3 Verbs):** For verbs that double themselves (like *dā* -\u003e *dadā*), Pāṇini writes a hard override: Rule 7.1.78 (*abhyastānām*). It completely bans the \u0027n\u0027 augment in all cases. \n    *   Root *dā* (give) + *śatṛ* \u003d *dadat*.\n    *   Nom. plural masculine is **dadataḥ** (it never becomes *dadantaḥ*).\n*   **The Feminine Generation:** To decline *śatṛ* participles in the feminine, Pāṇini attaches the feminine suffix **ṅīp** (long **ī**). But does it attach to the strong (*-ant*) or weak (*-at*) stem? Pāṇini maps this algorithmically:\n    *   Class 1, 4, 10 verbs *must* take the strong stem: **gacchantī** (the going woman).\n    *   Class 6 verbs are *optional*: **tudantī** OR **tudatī** (the striking woman).\n    *   Class 3 verbs *must* take the weak stem: **dadatī** (the giving woman).\n\n### 2. The Perfect Active Participles (*Kvasu* Affix)\nTo signify someone who *has* done something (e.g., \"having done\", \"having known\"), Pāṇini attaches the affix **kvasu**. This is one of the most volatile declension paradigms in the language, as it splits into **three different stems** depending on the case.\n\nTaking the root *kṛ* (to do), the raw base is **cakṛvas**.\n1.  **Strong Stem (Nom. all, Acc. sing/dual):** The stem lengthens and takes the \u0027n\u0027 augment, becoming **-vāṃs**. \n    *   Nom. singular: **cakṛvān** (he who has done).\n2.  **Weak Stem (Acc. plural, and all vowel-initial cases):** The \u0027v\u0027 undergoes vocalic implosion (*samprasāraṇa*) and collapses into a \u0027u\u0027, shifting the stem to **-uṣ**.\n    *   Accusative plural: **cakruṣaḥ** (those who have done).\n3.  **Middle Stem (all consonant-initial cases):** The \u0027s\u0027 converts to a \u0027t\u0027, making the stem **-vat**.\n    *   Instrumental plural: **cakṛvadbhiḥ**.\n\nFor the feminine, Pāṇini commands that the feminine suffix *ṅīp* (**ī**) must attach strictly to the **weak stem**. Therefore, the feminine form is always **cakruṣī** or **viduṣī** (she who has known).\n\n### 3. The Past Active Participles (*Ktavatup* Affix)\nTo create a standard past tense active participle (\"he went\", \"he ate\"), Pāṇini uses the affix **ktavatup**. The code letters drop, leaving **-tavat**.\n\nUnlike *kvasu*, this declension is highly regularized. It acts identically to nouns ending in *-vat* (like *bhagavat*).\n*   It toggles neatly between **-tavān** (strong, e.g., *kṛtavān*) and **-tavat** (weak, e.g., *kṛtavatā*).\n*   Unlike the chaotic *śatṛ* feminine rules, Pāṇini dictates that the feminine of *ktavatup* strictly takes the strong base + *ī*. Result: **kṛtavatī**. \n\n### 4. The \"Normalizers\": Past Passive (*Kta*) and Gerundives\nWhen Pāṇini wants to make a Past Passive Participle (\"was done\", \"was eaten\") or a Future Passive Participle/Gerundive (\"should be done\", \"must be eaten\"), he uses affixes that completely strip the complexity out of the declension.\n\n*   **Kta Affix (Past Passive):** Drops the \u0027k\u0027, leaves **-ta**. (e.g., *kṛta*, *gata*).\n*   **Tavya / Anīyar / Yat Affixes (Gerundives):** Leaves **-tavya**, **-anīya**, or **-ya** (e.g., *kartavya*, *karaṇīya*, *kārya*).\n\nThese affixes are structural \"normalizers.\" By ending in a short \u0027a\u0027, they force the verb to decline exactly like the most basic noun in the language (*rāma* for masculine, *phala* for neuter). \nFor the feminine, they take the suffix *ṭāp* (long **ā**), declining perfectly like the basic feminine noun *sītā* (e.g., *gatā*, *kartavyā*). \n\n### 5. The Present Middle/Passive Participles (*Śānac* Affix)\nFor verbs operating in the middle voice (Ātmanepada)—meaning actions done for oneself, or passive ongoing actions—Pāṇini uses the affix **śānac**. The dummy letters drop, leaving **-āna**. \n\nBecause *-āna* ends in a short \u0027a\u0027, these participles also decline wonderfully easily (like *rāma* / *sītā*). However, Pāṇini has a specific algorithmic rule for generating the base before declension happens: **The \u0027m\u0027 Augment (*muk*).**\n\nRule 7.2.82 (*āne muk*) commands that if the verb stem ends in a short \u0027a\u0027, you must insert the letter \u0027m\u0027 before the *-āna* suffix.\n*   Root *labh* (to get, Class 1) ends in \u0027a\u0027 -\u003e *labha*.\n*   *labha* + *āna* -\u003e inject \u0027m\u0027 -\u003e **labhamāna** (getting). \n*   Declines normally: *labhamānaḥ*, *labhamānam*, etc.\n\nBut if the