      "text": "If your focus is strictly on **\"What are all the different declension algorithms I actually have to code?\"**, then we need to move away from edge-case trivia and look at the language like a software architect. \n\nYou do not need to code thousands of different tables. You need to code exactly **19 distinct Declension Paradigms (Algorithm Paths)**. \n\nWhen your tool receives a base word, your router must send it to one of these 19 functions. If you build these 19 paths, your tool will cover 100% of the Sanskrit language.\n\nHere are the 19 declensions you must implement, including the final 4 specific paradigms we haven\u0027t fully detailed yet.\n\n---\n\n### GROUP 1: The Vowel Paradigms\n**1. The `A-Stem` Engine (`a`)**\n*   **Targets:** Masculine/Neuter words ending in short `a` (*Rāma, Phala*).\n*   **Logic:** Replaces *su/am* with *am* for Neuters; applies standard *ena, āya, āt, asya, e* endings.\n\n**2. The `Āp / Nadī` Engine (Long `ā, ī, ū` - Standard Feminines)**\n*   **Targets:** Standard feminine nouns (*Sītā, Nadī, Vadhū*).\n*   **Logic:** Injects the *āṭ* augment before vowel-suffixes (Dative *yai*, Abl/Gen *yāḥ*, Loc *yām*).\n\n**3. The `Ghi` Engine (Short `i, u`)**\n*   **Targets:** Masc/Fem/Neut ending in short `i, u` (*Hari, Guru, Mati, Vāri, Madhu*).\n*   **Logic:** Pops the vowel (*Guṇa*) in the Dat/Abl/Gen/Loc/Voc (*hareḥ, harau, hare*). For neuters, it strictly injects the *num* (\u0027n\u0027) augment (*vāriṇā*). \n\n**4. The `Root-Vowel` Engine (Monosyllabic `ā, ī, ū`)**\n*   **Targets:** Root-nouns (*Viśvapā, Dhī, Bhū*) and specific anomalies (*Senānī*).\n*   **Logic:** Forces the insertion of `iy` (for ī) and `uv` (for ū) before vowels. Masc \u0027ā\u0027 stems delete the \u0027ā\u0027 in weak cases (*viśvapaḥ*).\n\n**5. The `Ṛ-Stem` Engines (Doer vs. Family)**\n*   **Targets:** Words ending in `ṛ` (*Kartṛ, Pitṛ*).\n*   **Logic:** You must build a fork here. If it\u0027s a \"Doer\" (*kartṛ*), stretch to **ār** (*kartārau*). If it\u0027s \"Family\" (*pitṛ*), keep short **ar** (*pitarau*).\n\n**6. The `Diphthong` Engine (`e, ai, o, au`)**\n*   **Targets:** The rare vowel stems (*Go, Rai, Nau, Glau*).\n*   **Logic:** Follows standard Sandhi vowel-splits (`e -\u003e ay`, `o -\u003e av`, `au -\u003e āv`) before vowel suffixes.\n\n---\n\n### GROUP 2: The Consonant Paradigms\n**7. The `Standard Consonant` Engine**\n*   **Targets:** Pure consonants with no mutation (*Marut, Jagat*).\n*   **Logic:** Simply attaches the 21 *Sup* suffixes. Injects \u0027n\u0027 and stretches the preceding vowel for Neuter plurals (*jaganti*).\n\n**8. The `As / Is / Us` Sibilant Engine**\n*   **Targets:** Words ending in `s` (*Manas, Havis, Dhanus*).\n*   **Logic:** The \u0027s\u0027 must dynamically mutate into \u0027r\u0027, then into a visarga, then into an \u0027o\u0027 depending on the consonant suffix it hits (*manobhiḥ, manaḥsu*).\n\n**9. The `An-Stem` Engine (`an, man, van`)**\n*   **Targets:** *Rājan, Karman, Ātman*.\n*   **Logic:** Drops the \u0027n\u0027 in the Nom. singular. Drops the inner \u0027a\u0027 in the weak cases (*rājñā*), unless blocked by a heavy consonant cluster (*karmaṇā*).\n\n**10. The `In-Stem` Engine (`in, min, vin`)**\n*   **Targets:** *Yogin, Balin*.\n*   **Logic:** Acts exactly like a standard consonant in weak cases (*yoginā*), but completely drops the \u0027n\u0027 and acts like a vowel `i`-stem before consonant suffixes (*yogibhiḥ*).\n\n**11. The `At-Stem` Engine (`at, ant`)**\n*   **Targets:** Participles and adjectives (*Gacchat, Bhagavat, Mahat*).\n*   **Logic:** Toggles between a strong base containing \u0027n\u0027 (*gacchan*) and a weak base lacking \u0027n\u0027 (*gacchatā*).\n\n**12. The `Añc-Stem` Engine (Directional words)**\n*   **Targets:** *Pratyañc, Udañc*.\n*   **Logic:** The 3-tier collapse. Strong (*pratyaṅ*), Middle (*pratyag*), Weak (*pratīc*).\n\n**13. The `C, J, Ś, H` Root-Noun Engine**\n*   **Targets:** *Vāc, Rāj, Diś, Duh, Lih*.\n*   **Logic:** The base hardens into either a Guttural (`k/g`) or a Retroflex (`ṭ/ḍ`) depending on your hardcoded dictionary match before consonant suffixes.\n\n---\n\n### GROUP 3: The Pronoun \u0026 Number Paradigms\n**14. The `Sarva / Tad` Pronoun Engine**\n*   **Targets:** All standard pronouns. \n*   **Logic:** Intercepts standard suffixes and replaces them with *smai, smāt, smin, sām* (Masc) and *syai, syāḥ, syām* (Fem). Truncates `d` to `a` for *yad/tad*. \n\n**15. The `Ṣaṭ` Number Engine (5-19)**\n*   **Targets:** *Pañcan, Ṣaṣ, Aṣṭan*.\n*   **Logic:** Permanently deletes the Nominative and Accusative plural suffixes (`jas/śas`). Injects `nūṭ` for the Genitive plural (*pañcānām*).\n\n---\n\n### GROUP 4: The Final 4 \"Missing\" Paradigms\n*(You must build these last four specific engines, as they decline unlike anything else above).*\n\n**16. The `Kati` (How Many) Engine**\n*   **Targets:** Words ending in the *Dati* affix (`kati` \u003d how many, `tati` \u003d so many).\n*   **Logic:** Even though they end in `i`, they **strictly bypass the Ghi engine**. They are legally treated as *Ṣaṭ* (numbers). \n    *   They are locked to Plural. \n    *   They delete the Nom/Acc suffixes (*kati*). \n    *   They do NOT pop their vowel to \u0027e\u0027 (*katibhyaḥ*, NOT *katebhyaḥ*).\n\n**17. The `-Vas` (Kvasu) Adjective Engine**\n*   **Targets:** Perfect Active Participles (*Vidvas* \u003d scholar, *Pekivas* \u003d one who cooked).\n*   **Logic:** A highly volatile 3-stem shift engine.\n    *   Strong (Nom Sg): Stretches and takes \u0027n\u0027 -\u003e **vidvān**.\n    *   Middle (Inst Pl): The \u0027s\u0027 hardens to a \u0027t\u0027 -\u003e **vidvadbhiḥ**.\n    *   Weak (Acc Pl): The \u0027v\u0027 collapses into \u0027u\u0027 (*Samprasāraṇa*) -\u003e **viduṣaḥ** (Note the \u0027s\u0027 becomes \u0027ṣ\u0027). \n\n**18. The `Div` (Sky) Engine**\n*   **Targets:** The word `div` (Heaven/Sky).\n*   **Logic:** It has its own 3-tier algorithm replacing the \u0027v\u0027.\n    *   Strong (Nom Sg): \u0027v\u0027 becomes \u0027au\u0027 -\u003e **dyauḥ**.\n    *   Middle (Inst Pl): \u0027v\u0027 becomes \u0027u\u0027 -\u003e **dyubhiḥ**.\n    *   Weak (Acc Sg): Stays \u0027v\u0027 -\u003e **divam**.\n\n**19. The `Śvan / Yuvan` (Dog / Youth) Engine**\n*   **Targets:** *Śvan, Yuvan, Maghavan*.\n*   **Logic:** They look like `-an` stems, but they completely bypass the *An-Stem* engine. Instead of dropping their \u0027a\u0027, they undergo vocalic implosion (*Samprasāraṇa*).\n    *   Strong (Nom Sg): **śvā**, **yuvā**.\n    *   Weak (Inst Sg): The \u0027v\u0027 collapses into \u0027u\u0027 -\u003e **śunā**, **yūnā**.\n    *   Middle (Inst Pl): Acts like standard consonant -\u003e **śvabhiḥ**, **yuvabhiḥ**.\n\n---\n\n### The Final Checklist\nIf you build exactly **19 Class Objects / Switch-Case blocks** in your code representing these 19 algorithms, and you pass your input base-string into the correct one (using the flags we discussed: gender, root-noun, specific string matches), you will have successfully implemented the entirety of Sanskrit nominal morphology. \n\nThere are no other declensions. Every single word in the Sanskrit language mathematically maps to one of these 19 paths.",
